// A value object referenced ACROSS a context boundary — `Billing.Receipt.shipTo`
// resolves to `valueobject Addr` declared in the sibling context `Directory`,
// with nothing imported and nothing re-declared.
//
// Minted by the fixture-shape audit (docs/audits/2026-09-29-fixture-shape-coverage.md).
// NO model anywhere in this repo had this shape — not one of 431 fixtures,
// examples, journey stages or audit models — even though a type shared between
// two contexts is the ordinary DDD move and the README's own Quick Example
// shape.  `BoundedContextIR.siblingValueObjects` exists precisely to serve it,
// so every emitter that MATERIALISES a referenced VO has two lookup paths to
// keep in step: `ctx.valueObjects` for a local declaration and the sibling pool
// for a foreign one.  Nothing exercised the second.
//
// What it pins: the flattening must produce the SAME leaf columns whichever pool
// the VO came from.  `Directory.Person.home` and `Billing.Receipt.shipTo` are
// the same `Addr`, so `home_line1`/`home_geo_lat`/`home_geo_lng` and
// `ship_to_line1`/`ship_to_geo_lat`/`ship_to_geo_lng` must be structurally
// identical — same recursion, same leaf types, same NULLability.  `Addr.geo: Geo`
// keeps the nesting in play, because a cross-context lookup that succeeds at the
// first level and fails at the second is the likelier bug.
//
// The evaluation's F-008 is what the blindness cost: the consuming context
// emitted ONE column under the unflattened name while the owning context
// flattened correctly, so the migration and the ORM disagreed on column name AND
// type and only a live database could tell.  The root cause is shared by every
// emitter that materialises a VO: the CALL SITE comes from the aggregate's wire
// shape (which spans contexts) while the DEFINITION comes from a VO list, which
// must be the sibling pool (`findValueObjectInScope` / `valueObjectPool`), never
// the context's own `valueObjects` alone.  Every backend resolves it that way:
//
//   node    `shipTo_line1`/`shipTo_geo_lat`/`shipTo_geo_lng` in the drizzle
//           schema, the migration DDL and both rehydrate paths.
//   dotnet  `ReceiptConfiguration` nests `OwnsOne` and names `ship_to_line1` /
//           `ship_to_geo_lat` / `ship_to_geo_lng`.
//   java    `@AttributeOverride(name = "line1", column = @Column(name =
//           "ship_to_line1"))`, nested path overridden the same way.
//   elixir  `receipt_controller.ex` defines `serialize_addr/1` and the nested
//           `serialize_geo/1` for the sibling-context VO, as the owning
//           context's controller does.  A VO is one `:map` cell here, so there
//           is no flattening to get wrong — but a serializer is still an
//           artifact the VO needs, and only `mix compile --warnings-as-errors`
//           sees a missing one.  Grade a backend here by COMPILING it, never by
//           reading its emitted schema.
//   python  `receipt_repository.py` binds and reads the three flattened columns
//           `db/schema.py` and the migration create.  `mypy --strict` sees only
//           the hydrate site (`ReceiptRow` attribute access); the two bind sites
//           are untyped dict literals, so a regression there passes the compile
//           tier and fails only against a live database.
//
// COMPILE-TIER ONLY — no `test e2e` block yet.  A recorded behavioural case must
// ship a reviewed wire golden (`test/behavioral/wire-golden/`, gated by
// `golden-coverage.test.ts`) captured from a booted stack on every backend.
// That block is what would gate python's bind sites and the wire round trip
// through the sibling-pool lookup; its `gate-ledger.test.ts` entry falls when it
// lands.  What this fixture proves today is static and per-backend: the
// flattened column set the migration creates must equal the one the
// ORM/repository names, in BOTH contexts.
system VoCrossContext {
  subdomain Commerce {
    context Directory {
      valueobject Geo {
        lat: decimal
        lng: decimal
      }

      valueobject Addr {
        line1: string
        geo: Geo
      }

      aggregate Person with crudish {
        name: string
        home: Addr
        derived display: string = name
      }

      repository Persons for Person { }
    }

    // The consuming context.  `Addr` is NOT declared here.
    context Billing {
      aggregate Receipt with crudish {
        reference: string
        shipTo: Addr
        derived display: string = reference
      }

      repository Receipts for Receipt { }

      // The SECOND axis, crossed with the first.  `Shipper` is a TPH concrete
      // whose OWN required field is `Addr` — a value object declared in the
      // SIBLING context `Directory`, not here.  The audit matrix measured both
      // axes and found each covered only ALONE: `inheritance.ddd` puts a VO on
      // an inheriting aggregate's own field but declares the VO in the SAME
      // context, so the lookup never reaches `siblingValueObjects`; this file
      // exercised the sibling pool but had no `extends` at all, so the TPH
      // column path was never on it.  No corpus fixture crossed them.
      //
      // The crossing is what the subtype's column set depends on: a TPH
      // concrete's own fields become NULLABLE columns on the shared base
      // table, so the emitter narrows them back on read — and it must narrow
      // the FLATTENED leaves the sibling pool produces (`ship_line1`,
      // `ship_geo_lat`, `ship_geo_lng`), not the unflattened field name.  When
      // the narrowing reads the field and the columns come from the pool, the
      // two disagree and the row object has no such attribute.
      abstract aggregate Party inheritanceUsing: sharedTable {
        label: string
        derived display: string = label
      }

      aggregate Shipper extends Party with crudish {
        ship: Addr
      }

      repository Shippers for Shipper { }
    }
  }

  api CommerceApi from Commerce
  storage primary { type: postgres }
  resource directoryState { for: Directory, kind: state, use: primary }
  resource billingState { for: Billing, kind: state, use: primary }

  deployable d {
    platform: __PLATFORM__
    contexts: [Directory, Billing]
    dataSources: [directoryState, billingState]
    serves: CommerceApi
    port: 4000
  }
}
